us changed Georgia's seats from 6 to 7, Georgia still elected all of its members statewide, so it had no districts to redraw.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators"],"districtsChanged":0},"c020":{"why":"researched","kind":"other","text":"For the 1826 election only, Georgia elected its seven representatives from districts, created by an 1825 state law, instead of statewide. It returned to statewide elections in 1828.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1826_United_States_House_of_Representatives_elections_in_Georgia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_congressional_delegations_from_Georgia","https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://gahistoricnewspapers.galileo.usg.edu/lccn/sn82015108/1825-12-27/ed-1/seq-1/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c023":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"After the 1830 census changed Georgia's seats from 7 to 9, Georgia still elected all of its members statewide, so it had no districts to redraw.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators"],"districtsChanged":0},"c029":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1840 census, which cut Georgia's seats from 9 to 8. It was first used in the 1844 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":8},"c032":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Georgia's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1850 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c033":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1850 census, which left Georgia with 8 seats. It was first used in the 1852 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":8},"c037":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Georgia's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1860 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":8},"c040":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Georgia's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1866 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c043":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1870 census, which raised Georgia's seats from 7 to 9. It was first used in the 1872 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":9},"c048":{"why":"at-large","kind":"at-large","text":"After the 1880 census raised Georgia's seats from 9 to 10, the state kept its existing districts and elected one member statewide (at-large).","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators"],"districtsChanged":0},"c049":{"why":"researched","kind":"other","text":"Georgia's 10th seat, gained in the 1880 census, was filled statewide in 1882. Starting with the 1884 election, all 10 seats were elected from districts, which required new lines.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_congressional_delegations_from_Georgia","https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators"],"secondary":true,"districtsChanged":9},"c053":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1890 census, which raised Georgia's seats from 10 to 11. It was first used in the 1892 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":9},"c060":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Georgia's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1906 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":6},"c061":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Georgia's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1908 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":2},"c063":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1910 census, which raised Georgia's seats from 11 to 12. It was first used in the 1912 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":9},"c065":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Georgia's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1916 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":5},"c073":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1930 census, which cut Georgia's seats from 12 to 10. It was first used in the 1932 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":12},"c089":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1960 census, which left Georgia with 10 seats. It was first used in the 1964 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":9},"c093":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1970 census, which left Georgia with 10 seats. It was first used in the 1972 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":10},"c098":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1980 census, which left Georgia with 10 seats. It was first used in the 1982 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":10},"c103":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1990 census, which raised Georgia's seats from 10 to 11. It was first used in the 1992 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":11},"c105":{"why":"event","kind":"court","text":"New map following: Miller v. Johnson. The Supreme Court held that Georgia's 11th District was drawn with race as the predominant factor without sufficient justification, violating the Equal Protection Clause. A court-drawn map was used from the 1996 election.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/515/900","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/521/74"],"districtsChanged":10},"c108":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2000 census, which raised Georgia's seats from 11 to 13. It was first used in the 2002 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":13},"c110":{"why":"researched","kind":"mid-decade","text":"In 2005 the General Assembly passed a new congressional map replacing the one drawn in 2001, and Governor Sonny Perdue signed it on May 3, 2005. After Justice Department preclearance under the Voting Rights Act, it was first used in 2006.","sources":["https://rollcall.com/2005/05/03/perdue-signs-new-congressional-map/","https://electionlawblog.org/?p=4030"],"districtsChanged":13},"c113":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2010 census, which raised Georgia's seats from 13 to 14. It was first used in the 2012 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":14},"c115":{"why":"small","kind":"other","text":"Not a new map: the historical boundary records show only slight differences in one district from the previous Congress, and no statewide redistricting is recorded for this election.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":1},"c118":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2020 census, which left Georgia with 14 seats. It was first used in the 2022 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":14},"c119":{"why":"event","kind":"court","text":"New map following: Court-ordered redraw. A federal district court found in October 2023 that Georgia's 2021 congressional map violated Section 2 of the Voting Rights Act. The legislature passed a new map in December 2023, which the court approved and which was used from the 2024 election.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/georgia/"],"districtsChanged":9}}}